<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">My soul praise the Lord (John Dowland)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1592</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting in Thomas Est's The Whole Booke of Psalmes</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Herr, dich lob die Seele mein (Heinrich Schütz, SWV 202)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1628</span><span class="sg-chain-h">four-part setting of a metrical German text</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Es wartet alles auf dich (J. S. Bach, BWV 187)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1726</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first movement sets verses 27 and 28</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O Worship the King (Robert Grant)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1833</span><span class="sg-chain-h">hymn based on the psalm</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Psalm 104:4 (Miriam Shatal)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement for mixed chorus</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Akhnaten (Philip Glass)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">opera setting it in Hebrew</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Psalm 104 (William Lovelady)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cantata for Prince Philip's 75th birthday</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><a href="/songs/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">14th century BCE</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parallel text; Egyptian influence argued by some scholars, rejected by others</span></li></ul></section>
